WEBVTT


00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:05.250
Dealing with floats across the back
of our knitting can be a challenge,

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.350
but we do have lots of options.

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:12.420
Yarns strands that are carried
across the purl side of the

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.070
knitting are called floats.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:18.060
We can take advantage of floats
with the slip stitch technique.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:23.160
The strands are carried across the
knitting and form decorative patterns

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.170
against the purl side of the knitting.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:32.430
These examples are just one color
slip seen from the purl side.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.100
We'll come back to slip in a minute.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:40.020
When working with multiple strands of
yarn, especially when working with colors

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.440
per row, the floats can get problematic.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.480
In garments, we don't want long
floats that are easily snagged.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:51.960
So let's talk about some
ways of dealing with them.

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:58.650
First, if you're knitting a
jacket or a coat, a knitted or

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:01.170
fabric lining might be the answer.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:06.720
For example, by adding fabric
to a blanket, you could use

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:10.650
any multicolor stitch pattern
without worrying about floats.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:17.369
For a scarf knit it twice as
wide and fold it in half, then

00:01:17.369 --> 00:01:19.500
neatly seam up the long edge.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:24.360
And again, you can use beautiful Fair
Isle designs and hide the floats.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:30.899
Some knitters will latch up
long floats as they knit.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.470
Just hang the strands, every few rows.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:40.680
Please consider that this may distort
the stitches on either side of the float.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.140
But try it for yourself.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:47.220
This is a long-time favorite
method for many knitters

00:01:49.369 --> 00:01:54.560
Another technique that some knitters will
use after they're done knitting, is to

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.490
hand stitch those long floats in place.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:03.020
Just take another piece of yarn
and loosely stitch them down.

00:02:03.399 --> 00:02:07.635
You'll want to keep your stitching
loose and not distort the design.

00:02:09.824 --> 00:02:13.965
In this example, I'm hanging
the long float on a needle

00:02:14.055 --> 00:02:15.975
before I knit the next row.

00:02:16.425 --> 00:02:19.125
I'm taking care to keep the
needle in working position

00:02:19.245 --> 00:02:21.395
just looping the strand over.

00:02:23.885 --> 00:02:28.875
When I knit the next row, that strand
just gets woven in and it doesn't knit.

00:02:31.185 --> 00:02:34.635
Another idea is to modify
the stitch pattern a bit.

00:02:35.535 --> 00:02:39.375
Reduce the floats for this
snowman by adding some snow in

00:02:39.375 --> 00:02:45.325
the background, just a few random
stitches can break up long floats.

00:02:50.325 --> 00:02:54.279
This stitch pattern might be
better worked as Intarsia.

00:02:54.899 --> 00:02:58.950
Isolating just the little foot
without carrying the stitches

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:00.450
to the edge of the knitting.

00:03:00.899 --> 00:03:02.100
Makes more sense.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:05.489
Instead of dealing with those
long floats on the ends.

00:03:07.815 --> 00:03:10.905
Here I am canceling the
end needle selection.

00:03:10.935 --> 00:03:15.165
So the contrasting yarn isn't
carried to the edge stitches.

00:03:16.665 --> 00:03:21.555
Since the working yarn is traveling along
the edge of the motif, instead of the

00:03:21.555 --> 00:03:27.315
edge of the knitting, you'll need to be
sure to use a separate strand of yarn

00:03:27.375 --> 00:03:32.745
and wrap the stitches between the motif
and the background to prevent holes.

00:03:35.894 --> 00:03:37.545
Back to slip stitch.

00:03:37.845 --> 00:03:44.864
To add some color to your knitting without
floats, consider multicolor slip or tuck.

00:03:45.644 --> 00:03:51.135
Multicolor slip is basically stripes
where selected needles are slipped and

00:03:51.135 --> 00:03:53.625
the color is carried to another row.

00:03:54.495 --> 00:04:00.470
In this example, the peach color from
this row doesn't knit for the four rows

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:06.200
of the turquoise, the peach stitches
are carried over those turquoise rows,

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:09.530
creating a two color stitch
pattern with no floats.

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:13.790
The knit side is often
used for this technique.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:18.970
Now you won't be knitting, cute
little snowman or snowflakes, but

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:23.320
this is a method of adding color
without dealing with floats at all.

00:04:25.695 --> 00:04:27.635
Back to the lining idea.

00:04:27.965 --> 00:04:30.485
Here's a blanket technique
for you to consider.

00:04:30.965 --> 00:04:33.575
I knit a blanket like this years ago.

00:04:33.875 --> 00:04:35.735
I don't remember who was the designer.

00:04:35.735 --> 00:04:40.055
I don't have the original stitch
pattern, but this idea can also be

00:04:40.055 --> 00:04:41.974
applied to other stitch patterns.

00:04:43.784 --> 00:04:46.935
First, I knit the crayon
panel in Fair Isle.

00:04:47.414 --> 00:04:50.055
As you can imagine, there
were lots of floats.

00:04:51.854 --> 00:04:55.094
Let's say each crayon section is 40 rows.

00:04:55.545 --> 00:04:59.805
When the panel was complete, I didn't
bind off, but I continued knitting

00:04:59.805 --> 00:05:02.115
the black color to create a lining.

00:05:03.224 --> 00:05:04.724
I knit 40 rows.

00:05:05.474 --> 00:05:10.094
I picked up the edge of that
first crayon, knit the row.

00:05:11.054 --> 00:05:12.885
Joining the two pieces together.

00:05:13.815 --> 00:05:16.005
I knit another 40 rows of lining.

00:05:16.575 --> 00:05:19.834
Picked up the stitches from
the edge of the next crayon.

00:05:20.354 --> 00:05:21.525
Knit the row.

00:05:22.065 --> 00:05:24.315
Again, joining the two pieces together.

00:05:25.364 --> 00:05:30.585
I continued doing this until I got to the
cast on edge and the lining was complete.

00:05:31.635 --> 00:05:33.554
In other words, I made a sandwich.

00:05:33.945 --> 00:05:38.325
Joining at regular intervals,
making a double warm blanket.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:39.930
And hiding the floats.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:43.350
You could do this with any stitch pattern.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:47.010
Think of this as quilting,
where you're joining the two

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.560
layers of knitting as you knit.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:56.520
We have options when knitting and color
incorporate some of these techniques

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:59.630
and don't avoid color because of floats.

